Chapter 10, Section 27: "The Sun That Does Not Rise from the West"



"How does one successfully escape from a castle, I wonder?"


Had those words been uttered in a faint, mosquito-like whisper, Heinkel would have taken them as Filoere's moment of weakness—the exhausted lament of someone worn down by constant opposition.

He might have even felt relieved that she, who always gave everything her all, had a heart that could falter too.

But—


"Sorry. Say that again."


"I said! How does one successfully escape from a castle, I wonder?"


"—. So I didn't mishear after all..."


Those crimson eyes had lost none of their strength, and Filoere's voice rang with vigor. Her entire being showed not a trace of the frailty of someone broken by adversity.

She did seem aware, at least, that this was not a conversation to be overheard—her voice was lowered—but the rebellious spirit brimming in her eyes and voice was utterly unconcealed.

Where had the demureness she had displayed before Teiga and the others just moments ago vanished to?


"That was, of course, to lull those two into letting their guard down. If I were to bring up something like this, neither Teiga nor Sakura would ever listen seriously."


"In that case, the same condition applies to me, doesn't it? Why would you think I'd listen..."


"Because, Heinkel-sama, you are my knight, are you not? You are not on the side of the Divine Dragon Church—you are on my side! ...Isn't that right?"


"This isn't the kind of thing you confide in someone while sounding unsure of the answer yourself."


As Filoere began tilting her head mid-sentence, Heinkel scratched at his head vigorously.

Taking her seriously would be absurd, but he could tell she had given this genuine thought before speaking. And if he were to cut her off before she could fully voice it, there was no telling what reckless action she might undertake on her own.

The key was to go along with the conversation and let her vent.


"First of all, regarding this escape plan... In short, the people you want to help escape are Lady Felt and Lady Emilia, correct?"


"Eh? Yes, exactly! Oh, thank goodness. I was sure you were about to betray me—my heart was pounding! But if you're on board too, Heinkel-sama, then there's nothing to fear!"


"—. And what kind of plan have you come up with?"


There was no way he was actually on board with such a foolish scheme, but he prompted her onward without interrupting.

Though it had only been a short while, what Heinkel had learned from his association with Filoere was this: if he stopped to address every questionable part of her statements, there would never be enough hours in a day.

Utterly unaware of her knight's inner thoughts, Filoere laughed, "Fufufu, I'm so glad you asked," and stealthily produced a folded piece of paper from within her clothes.


"Of course, I am not speaking on a mere whim. These past few days, I have been diligently investigating the castle in my own way."


As she spoke, Filoere cleared the pile of reply letters from the desk and spread the paper out upon it.

It was a hand-drawn floor plan of the royal castle—sections Filoere had accessed during prayers and petitions, recorded with her own feet and eyes. It detailed the areas where visitors and healers were permitted entry, doors restricted to castle personnel only—even from the perspective of Heinkel, who was nominally the vice-captain of the Royal Guard, it was impressively detailed.

Furthermore, the handwritten map used different colored inks, including annotations about the number of knights she had spotted during each visit, the timing of guard rotations, and other notes on the security network. It was the product of espionage activities far more meticulous than Heinkel had ever imagined—impossible to explain away.


"Y-you... this is..."


"How is it? Do you understand my seriousness now? Incidentally, by eavesdropping on the conversations of the civil officials who deliver my letters and the castle staff, I've also learned that Emilia and Felt are being confined in the eastern wing of the castle. Impressive, isn't it!"


"What the hell are you thinking, you fool!"


Heinkel shouted at Filoere, who clearly had no grasp of the gravity of the situation, then snatched up the handwritten map and crushed it in his fist. Filoere let out a cry of "Ah—!" but he paid no heed. If something like this were discovered, it would be a catastrophe.

Here was proof that Filoere—a royal selection candidate and the "Saint" of the Divine Dragon Church—had harbored rebellious intentions against the royal castle. And what made it truly damning was that it was all real.


"Listen to me. Forget about this thing. Forget the whole plan. In the first place, it would never work. And even if it did, what comes after that?"


It was a plan born of childish petulance, not even worth discussing in terms of feasibility.

True, Felt and Emilia were currently in difficult positions, deprived of their freedom. Filoere's appeals had been obstructed at every turn by the royal castle and the church—she too was constrained beyond measure.

But that was all. This situation could not last forever. The royal selection had a definite deadline, and prolonged chaos in the capital would also mean the erosion of authority for both the Council of Elders and the royal castle.


"That's why now is the time to keep your head low and endure. If you do that..."


"—If I do that, then what? Are you saying that if we simply sit and wait for time to pass, the clouds hanging over our heads will disperse, and the order of tomorrow will change?"


It was Filoere's quiet voice that cut through Heinkel, who had been muttering as though squeezing the words out while crushing the map smaller and smaller in his hand.

She, whose work had been literally crushed in his fist, showed no sign of heartbreak on her face. Instead, she fixed her gaze steadily upon Heinkel.


"Heinkel-sama, even if we remain obedient, the sun will not rise from the west. We must act. Prayer is a sacred and noble act, but if one chooses it as the first and last resort, even the wings of the Dragon will break under the weight."


"—"


"For the past month and a half, I believed I was doing everything I could. But if I give up here, I will feel as though all that time, I was merely making excuses for myself. As if I was doing it just to comfort myself and Emilia and the others—to hear them say, 'It didn't work out, but thank you for trying.' ...That is not my intention!"


With that final word spoken loudly, Filoere struck the wall of the room with her hand. There, carved into the wall, was the same magnificent emblem of the Divine Dragon that adorned various parts of the royal castle—the symbol that the Kingdom revered.

The Divine Dragon Church likewise worshipped, praised, and believed in the same entity as the Dragon Kingdom.

Filoere was a royal selection candidate and the Church's "Saint"—one could even say she was an existence recognized by both the Kingdom and the Divine Dragon Church.

Yet the will she now displayed was unmistakably one that defied both.


"Do you remember, Heinkel-sama? I want to create a world where I can extend my hand to anyone seeking salvation. If I become king, that is my goal. Anyone—anyone at all. That is the crucial part."


"—"


"If I, who proclaim such things, do not extend my hand to Emilia and Felt in their hour of need, then who could ever believe in me? Even I could not believe in myself. Words must be accompanied by actions."


"...Do you understand what that means?"


It must feel good to boldly declare one's desires without shame.

It might even be called heroic—if only she truly understood the meaning and value of what she would be casting aside.


"If you defy both the castle and the church, then what exactly do you become? You are a royal selection candidate. You are the 'Saint.' That is why people lend their ears to your words."


No matter how miserable and contemptible a person might be, because one is of the Sword Saint lineage, because one is the vice-captain of the Royal Guard, because one is the biological father of the strongest Sword Saint in history—no one can ignore them.

If she threw that away, then who would listen to the words of someone who was nothing—


"—I have you, Heinkel-sama."


—Filoere said it as though she had the support of ten thousand soldiers.


Still touching the Dragon emblem on the wall, she made a decision that might mean turning her back on the very faith to which she had dedicated her prayers—and she announced it to Heinkel, who wore the uniform of the Church that enshrined it.

She did not understand how utterly foolish this decision was.

She did not understand—and yet—


"...I really shouldn't have become your knight."


Setting aside matters of position or utility, Heinkel regretted having taken Filoere's hand.

Because of that, he had been forced to listen to this utterly worthless consultation.

And having heard it, he was now forced to help a girl who had no one else to rely on but him.


'—Congratulations.'


The words of congratulation Reinhard had offered him in the castle corridor echoed in his ears.

Reinhard had celebrated Heinkel's selection as Filoere's knight. He didn't think it had been a lie or empty flattery. Reinhard would not engage in such duplicity.

But was it really so? Was this truly something to celebrate?

If being chosen as someone's knight, if deciding to entrust one's sword to another, was a joyous and wonderful thing—then why did his stomach and chest ache so, why did his heart and soul have to groan in agony?


"Heinkel-sama? So, um, about what we were discussing..."


Filoere hesitantly spoke, facing Heinkel who had covered his face with his palm and fallen silent.

Having said what she needed to say, she was now seeking the answer—the direction this conversation would take.

This was a girl who had written a hundred letters, only to be endlessly deferred. She was sick to death of waiting. Her heart was surely racing, desperate for an immediate answer.

It was cruel to Filoere, but Heinkel's answer was—


"—Half a month."


"Eh?"


"Sneaking into a castle is something you do with far more seriousness and dedication than this."


As he spoke, Heinkel spread the crumpled, hand-drawn map back onto the desk. He smoothed out the wrinkles with his palm and glared at the plan—a plan its creator had surely made in complete earnestness, yet riddled with holes.

As it stood, it was utterly unusable.


"At minimum, we need half a month for preparation and refining the plan. The castle guards and patrol routes are bound to be reviewed and adjusted periodically. The information Lady Filoere has gathered won't be usable as-is. You don't want to be the fool who gets caught because he swallowed outdated intelligence, do you?"


"—! Does that mean...!"


"Stop making that face. I think you're a fool, Lady Filoere."


"Isn't that fine? You're going along with this fool, so you must be quite the fool yourself, Heinkel-sama! We'll be a pair of great fools together, master and servant!"


"A fool cannot become king. You'll be throwing away your position and your honor."


At Heinkel's response, Filoere's expression brightened visibly. As she looked ready to burst into a song of joy at any moment, Heinkel once again confronted her with reality.

Nothing had been done yet. Right now, she could still turn back.


"The most foolish part of this plan is that whether it succeeds or fails, everyone involved will be worse off. Naturally, Lady Filoere will lose her position as a royal selection candidate. The Divine Dragon Church will be suspected of plotting rebellion against the Kingdom, and the entire church will come under investigation. Teiga and Sakura will likely be pressed to confess everything they know."


Of course, the Divine Dragon Church had its own opaque aspects. Heinkel didn't believe that Filoere's single action would expose all of that darkness, but what was needed here was persuasiveness.


"If we act now, it will become harder to reach out to those we could have saved in the future. Permission to use the sacraments on Priestella's victims will never be granted again. The revocation of your 'Saint' designation would truly come to pass. It would be a complete negation of everything Lady Filoere has done until now. Even so—"


"—I will do it."


"—"


"I will do it regardless. The wish I hold was not something that came to me because I am a royal selection candidate or because I am the 'Saint.' And besides, even if we wait..."


"The sun won't rise from the west, huh."


"Yes!"


Filoere pointed sharply at his face, and Heinkel lowered her finger with his hand, admonishing, "Too loud." She hastily covered her mouth with both hands, but the sight only drew a sigh from him.

How had it come to this? He couldn't understand himself. All he knew was that, faced with Filoere's passionate determination, he found it difficult to release the hand he had once taken.

—Do I still want to play at being a knight?


It was a question he had asked himself before, and once again, Heinkel could not answer it himself.



△▼△▼△▼△



—For the next half month, Heinkel dredged up his memories from his time as the largely decorative vice-captain of the Royal Guard, scraping the bottom of that old barrel like searching for the last dregs of wine.


He secretly removed old castle maps stored in the knight order's archives—copies of emergency evacuation routes—and overlaid them with Filoere's handwritten floor plan, contemplating viable escape routes.

He accompanied Filoere on her 110th and 120th direct appeals, recording knight positions and the rotation times that synchronized with the bell tolls, grasping the hours when the guard presence thinned. He compared the individual capabilities and morale of the knights against his own memories, selecting an appropriate day for the operation.


"Zeol is a detestable man who flaunts his pedigree at every turn, but lately he's been entrusted with the important eastern wing. ...Did he curry favor with someone?"


He was a man who specialized in wielding his knightly status and family name as weapons, rather than relying on swordsmanship or professional conscience.

In short, he could be called an inferior version of Heinkel. Though when it came to abandoning and neglecting knightly duties, Heinkel and Zeol were beyond comparison. At least Zeol, if not competently, performed his knightly work properly.

The only reason Heinkel had not been expelled from the knight order was purely the power of his family name.


"That said, we can't build a schedule assuming someone will fall asleep on duty."


The fact that Zeol and his cronies were guarding the eastern wing where Felt and Emilia—who should have been high-priority prisoners—were confined meant that the castle considered that level of security sufficient.

In other words, the captive Felt and the others must have been behaving very obediently. Or perhaps they had been given reasons that forced them to behave.

And that applied not just to Felt and her group, but exactly the same to Reinhard, who was obediently following the royal castle's orders and staying away from his liege.


"...In the worst case, even if Lady Filoere goes waltzing in to visit them, the other side might be the ones to brush her hand away."


If Felt and the others were complying with their confinement due to some hidden circumstances, then even if they were presented with a fully prepared escape plan, they might not nod in agreement.

In that case, the ladder wouldn't just be pulled away—it would be set ablaze beneath their feet. They'd either sit and be burned alive, or leap to a likely death. Death or broken bones would be unavoidable.

However, with direct meetings with the prisoners forbidden, it was difficult even to uncover whatever hidden circumstances might exist.


"I don't want to gamble blindly with such a crucial unknown left unaddressed..."


Half a month was the deadline Heinkel himself had proposed—though of course, if the risks became too great, extending the period would be the natural thing to do.

But even if an indefinite grace period were granted, a plan without a single gap would never emerge.


—During the half month Heinkel spent on preparations, Filoere's efforts were remarkable.


Needless to say, Filoere was a girl who was terrible at lying.

If she had any scheme, it would show plainly in her attitude and words, and her inner thoughts would immediately come under suspicion. He had worried she would never be able to hide it from Teiga and Sakura, who had known her for so long.

But Filoere dispelled those worries, performing her usual self so convincingly that even Heinkel, who knew the truth, was nearly deceived. She carried on without letting anything show.


She performed her daily routines, continued her appeals for the interrogation of the two prisoners, her petitions for permission to use the sacraments on the people of Priestella, writing letters, making direct appeals—she never stopped. And each time she was put off, she genuinely wept and raged, staying true to herself.

The consistency of her behavior made it clear that she was strongly steeling herself, bolstered by the fact that Heinkel had set a deadline and agreed with her thinking.

He could not betray that trust.


As Heinkel himself had predicted, the necessary preparations were completed within the half month.

The pretext for entering the castle, the hour when the guard presence thinned, the diversion to draw attention elsewhere, the route to reach the captive Felt and the others, the escape route to lead them out—and the evacuation of those whose continued presence in the capital would constitute a vulnerability.

The recklessness on paper had been refined over half a month into something that required only courage as the fare to entrust to fate. —And, as it happened, the day fell exactly two months after Heinkel and Filoere had sworn their oath as master and servant.




—Over the capital's sky, just beginning to lighten, the bell announcing morning prayers was ringing.


Having completed her morning prayers as usual and fulfilled her duties as the "Saint," Filoere stood before the main gate of the royal castle, holding her 150th petition in hand.


"...Adding fifty more letters in half a month is excessive."


"Whatever do you mean? A nice round number feels auspicious, doesn't it? This is a plan that Heinkel-sama has refined and refined. I will spare no effort to help make it succeed!"


"What kind of effort is that, exactly?"


As always, the direction of her efforts was somewhat lost, but her spirit seemed full.

Indeed, in stark contrast to Heinkel, whose mood grew heavier as the day approached, Filoere's complexion had only improved—he felt the difference between a true performer and himself.

At any rate—


"Are you nervous?"


"Yes! My heart feels like it might leap out of my mouth at any moment!"


"If it does, swallow it back down. Absolutely do not drop it inside the castle."


"Of course. It would be troublesome if someone picked it up, and pitiful for whoever did."


The exchanged banter was in their usual rhythm, and he marveled at her nerve.

Then, resolving not to be the one to hold them back, Heinkel dragged his heavy determination forward and raised his face to look at the castle.

Through his duties as vice-captain of the Royal Guard, and as Filoere's knight escorting her, he had visited the royal castle many times. —But sneaking into the castle with a scheme in mind was something Heinkel had not done in fifteen years. This would be his second time.


"I entrusted Teiga and Sakura with proper errands. They've been busy securing routes to the treatment centers until today. They shouldn't suspect anything."


"It was fortunate that their business wrapped up in exactly half a month too. —Up to this point, we can still turn back, you know?"


"There's no need to test my resolve so many times. The scriptures say: 'The wise unravel the complex; the holy revere the simple. Thus the Dragon's law is concise: to love, to protect, to believe. No deeper wisdom exists to rival this.'!"


"Those aren't the words of someone about to defy that very church."


Heinkel shrugged at Filoere, who so brazenly quoted the teachings of the Divine Dragon Church—the very church she was about to betray—as though a part of her creed were undeniably rooted in it.

There was no turning back now. As if to prove it—


"—Thank you for waiting. Please, come through."


The gate guard, having confirmed their reason for visiting and their identities, bowed and opened the way beyond the heavy gate. Filoere stepped forward, and Heinkel stepped out a half-pace ahead of her.

Using the petition that was expected to be rejected as a cover, the master and her knight boldly walked through the front entrance—to break into the royal castle.



△▼△▼△▼△



—The plan proceeded at a breakneck pace, so smoothly it was almost unnerving.


"All of this is the result of the good deeds that Emilia and I have accumulated daily. Heinkel-sama's constant self-deprecation and bad behavior must be offset by them!"


So declared Filoere, welcoming the situation that matched their prior arrangements exactly.

It was a harsh assessment, but since things were actually going well, he hesitated to argue. Besides, it would be strange to complain about things proceeding according to plan.


On this day as well, Filoere visited the Council of Elders and upper nobles to appeal for the interrogation hearings, using that as her pretext—and was, as expected, carelessly dismissed with empty responses.

While consoling Filoere, whose frustration was not an act but genuine, Heinkel used the pretense of calming her down to slip away from watchful eyes—and infiltrate the planned route.


"—? Heinkel?"


Avoiding the eyes of patrolling guards, they reached the depths of the eastern wing. Before the target room stood two guards, one of whom was the long-haired knight Zeol Zandrick—a man who had long treated Heinkel with open hostility, and whom Heinkel likewise disliked.

Zeol and the other, a sycophantic knight, spotted Heinkel walking briskly down the corridor with Filoere behind him, and their eyes widened in shock.


"Why are you here..."


"Because it's your shift."


Zeol stood frozen in confusion. That single instant was enough.

Heinkel dropped low, vanishing from his opponent's line of sight, then seized the wide-eyed man's wrist and pulled him in, driving a throat-strike into his exposed neck to silence him.

As Zeol let out a choked "Gah—", Heinkel swept his legs out from under him, and before the remaining knight could draw his sword, Heinkel drove the pommel of his own sword into the man's solar plexus, then finished him with a follow-up blow that sent him to the floor.

Then he turned back to Zeol, who was on his knees clutching his throat.


"Have you... lost your... mind..."


"I've been meaning to say this for a while: if you're a knight, cut your hair or tie it back."


Heinkel dismissed the gasping, breathless Zeol with those parting words, then struck him on the back of the head, rendering both guards unconscious.

After that, he let out a long breath. —Now, there was truly no turning back.


"I'll count it as a small mercy that it was Zeol..."


Even so, beating down a knight who was faithful to his duties weighed on his conscience.

Taking what comfort he could in the fact that it had been a man he had reason to dislike, Heinkel moved the two unconscious bodies to the side of the corridor. "Lady Filoere," he called out.


"No one should come for a while, but this is the crucial part. How quickly we can persuade Lady Felt and Lady Emilia... What's wrong?"


"N-no, it's just... I've never seen you fight before, Heinkel-sama, so I was, well, trembling with amazement. My knight is strong!"


"—. I simply struck when my opponent's guard was down. A strong knight wouldn't have been caught off guard in the first place."


That, Heinkel thought, was the difference between a counterfeit with only a title and the genuine article.

If a lucky surprise attack succeeded and she mistook that for Heinkel's true ability, it would only endanger Filoere herself.

Having admonished Filoere thus—


"—"


He slowly opened the door and peered inside.

Though it was effectively confinement, the official pretense was protection. He judged there was no need to worry about guards inside the room, but Heinkel kept his guard up, Filoere behind him.

Seeing Heinkel's face, the person in the room stared with wide-eyed surprise.


"...Well, ain't this an unexpected face. Just so you know, I haven't seen your son's face in a while either."


"—. I know that without you telling me. We've been passing each other, more or less."


Having Reinhard brought up in the very first exchange, Heinkel answered with a sour expression.

Of course, this was predictable. The only common topic between Heinkel and her—Felt—would naturally be his son.

As they exchanged that brief exchange, Filoere, unable to contain herself, poked her face out from beside Heinkel.


"—Heinkel-sama, is it all right? Is it safe now? Is anyone there?"


Whether she had any sense of caution or not, Filoere literally stuck her neck into the room. Meeting Filoere's eyes, Felt made an openly displeased face.


"Just when I thought being locked up was annoying enough, now this..."


"Hey, that's beyond disrespectful. Lady Filoere went out of her way to come see you."


Honestly, Felt's reaction was expected—and he even sympathized with it—but Heinkel offered the rebuke as a clear statement of position and as part of his role as a knight.

However, Filoere interjected, "Now, now, now."


"It's fine, Heinkel-sama! Arguing with Felt about that would be fruitless for both of us. More importantly, we should have a substantive discussion!"


"You're too loud. A substantive discussion?"


Felt chided Filoere's volume and looked between the two of them with probing eyes.

Apparently, she had caught the implication behind how he addressed Filoere. As always, despite her rough upbringing and crude attitude, she was perceptive. Perhaps it was the blood of the Lugunica royal family. —No, if it were Lugunica royal blood, Filoere's lack of defensiveness seemed more fitting.

As Heinkel's thoughts wandered to such irrelevant musings—


Filoere nodded vigorously at Felt's words.


"Of course, it's a substantive discussion! An abundant harvest! Actually, I thought you might be reaching the end of your patience about now, Felt. Because I am too! So, am I right?"


"...Well, I won't deny it. But that you've also reached your limit—"


"—Let's break out of prison. You, me, Emilia—all of us together."


Straightforward, without detours—truly, a direct frontal assault of a proposal.

At that audacious suggestion, Felt silently looked up at the ceiling and fell quiet for a moment.


"First Emilia-nee, now Filoere... What's with royal selection candidates?"


"Eh, what do you mean!?"


"Nah, it's just that the Emilia-nee in my head was also eagerly getting ready to bust out of here. I was wondering how your thinking overlapped so perfectly."


"Is it... because Emilia and I are best friends? Oh my... Felt, to think you'd see us that way..."


"Oi, old man. Don't tell me you left the whole plan to this flower garden?"


Felt, exasperated by Filoere pressing her hands to her reddened cheeks, turned the conversation toward Heinkel.

Given their relationship, she probably had no desire to exchange words with Heinkel either, but he had to admire her willingness to grasp even a soiled tool when the situation required it.


"I won't go so far as to say 'rest easy,' but I've spent a fair amount of time on preparations. I've confirmed the number of guards and patrol routes. The escape path is an old royal family evacuation route in the castle. I've arranged a hideout for after we get out, and the means to leave the capital."


"Heh, you're more capable than I expected."


"See, Heinkel-sama is quite capable! But but, I was the one who first thought of the escape, so please don't forget that!"


"That's not something to boast about; it's something to reflect on."


"Ehhhh!?"


Why she thought she'd be praised for that, he had no idea—Filoere pouted at the unexpected response.

Beside her, Felt crossed her slender arms and thought for a moment, tapping her arm with her finger. After about ten seconds, she nodded, "All right."


"Fine, I'll go along with it. I was planning to make a move soon anyway. Though my plan was just to rough up whoever brought the food, steal their keys and sword, and run. If I found Emilia-nee along the way, great—but it was all going to be improvised."


"Improvised doesn't count as a plan..."


"I had no tools and no info, what else was I supposed to do? And now those tools and info grew legs and came to me. No way I'm passing that up."


"See, see, I knew Felt would eagerly agree! Didn't I tell you, Heinkel-sama? I like this about her."


"I'm starting to not dislike that about you too."


Matching Filoere's cheerful energy, Felt flashed a grin that showed her fang-like tooth.

From prior information alone, these two should have had a complicated relationship with every reason to be at each other's throats—but it seemed they had found common ground in their willingness to use any means necessary for a greater cause.


"This is going to give me a headache later, but for now it's fine. Then follow me. Lady Emilia is on a higher floor. They've made appropriate preparations to seal her magic—"


"—What about Reinhard?"


"—"


Just as he was about to move things forward now that agreement had been reached, the question he had known was coming finally came, and Heinkel's words caught in his throat.


"Does that bastard know about this? Or is he waiting outside?"


She feigned a casual question, but Felt's voice was slightly lower than before.

For over two months, Felt and Reinhard had not seen each other's faces—Heinkel and the others had heard as much during their previous encounter.

Naturally, this confinement was not what Felt wanted. How did she feel about the knight who had left his liege in such an unwilling position?


"He doesn't know. That guy knows nothing about this."


"—"


"I didn't involve him in this plan. Besides, if word of this had reached his ears, neither Lady Filoere nor I would have made it here."


That was neither sarcasm nor spite—it was Heinkel's honest feeling.


"...Is that so."


Felt surely understood the implications of Heinkel's answer, but she responded with only those brief words, showing neither disappointment nor dejection on her face.

Instead, when her closed eyelids opened again, what dwelt in those crimson eyes was flame—a dazzling, blazing flame of anger.

Feeding that passion to the fire, she laughed boldly.


"Then all the more reason I can't stay in a place like this. I want to wreck that bastard's smug face, so let's get the hell out of here."


"—"


"What, got a problem?"


"—. No."


Heinkel spat out the words, careful not to let the emotion that had welled up in his chest be seen through.

Honestly, he didn't understand. Who had linked Reinhard to this girl? With what intention, and what outcome had been hoped for—he understood none of it.

But now, he was made to think that if Reinhard were to choose something, perhaps it could only have been this girl. —Or perhaps it was the reverse.

Perhaps it was Reinhard who had been chosen.

If so—


"That's the spirit! At this rate, we'll snatch Emilia right out, and Heinkel-sama's divine strategies will handle the rest!"


"—"


The profound sentiment that had been passing through his chest was rudely chopped apart by Filoere's bottomlessly cheerful voice.

Taken aback by her statement—the very epitome of relying on others—the tension drained from his shoulders.

Brooding over pointless things at pointless moments was Heinkel's innate bad habit. It felt as though someone had kicked him in the back, telling him it was all futile.


"You're pretty trusted, huh. Is this really the same old man I know?"


"Same one. If it comes down to it, I'll put my sword to your neck and force my way through with you as a hostage."


"Ha, yeah, that sounds like the old man I know."


"Why are you two finding common ground through such dangerous talk?"


Leaving Filoere tilting her head in confusion, Heinkel checked the corridor—confirming nothing had been discovered yet—and jerked his chin for the two of them to follow.

Fortunately, contrary to his pessimistic expectations, persuading Felt had gone smoothly. There was considerable time remaining before the guard rotation. —He felt a sense of confidence. This could work.


"—"


He glanced back at Filoere and Felt following behind.

Golden hair and crimson eyes—two girls bearing the same features, who also shared the same title of royal selection candidate. There had to be a reason they had been chosen and put forward for such positions. A reason that fate favored them. Perhaps they truly possessed it.


"Watch your footsteps and follow me."


Issuing instructions that were unnecessary for Felt but essential for Filoere, Heinkel and the others made their way toward Emilia's confinement room on the upper floor.

