Still, there was a ray of hope. —The hope that Reinhard would not come.


"Confining the royal selection candidates together in the same wing was their mistake."


The eastern wing should now be the most heavily guarded part of the royal castle, but the Kingdom could not station Reinhard—their strongest card—in this wing, of all places.

That was, in itself, the result of fearing Felt's influence over Reinhard.

By having Felt as an intermediary, the Kingdom could not help but harbor a sliver of unease about using Reinhard at will. —That was what kept Reinhard away from the eastern wing.

With the capital's unrest not yet fully quelled, Reinhard was once again assigned today to the defense of the capital's outer walls, using the pretext of maintaining public order.

Heinkel could say without exaggeration that more than half of the half-month spent on preparation had been devoted to achieving this certainty. —Therefore, Reinhard would not come.


"Halt."


They arrived at the target floor, and Heinkel peered down the corridor from the edge of the stairwell, stopping the two behind him.

