Before the unnerved Filoere, Heinkel and Felt's opinions aligned.

While Filoere muttered behind him, "But he's my knight...," Heinkel suppressed a click of his tongue.

It was strange to say, but having guards present as expected would have been easier on his nerves.

What should be there is not. What ought to exist does not. —A discrepancy from expectations, even when it worked in their favor as luck, squeezed Heinkel's stomach like a vice.

When coincidences pile up at the gambling table, only an amateur rejoices at their luck.

The seasoned loser suspects they are being allowed to win.


"—Stay here."


Heinkel instructed the two to remain where they were and finished climbing the stairs.

Then, slowly, he stepped into the corridor, closing the distance one step, two steps, toward where the guards should have been standing. The silence rang sharp against his eardrums, and his heartbeat was hot and painful.

Whether that was phantom pain born of cowardice or something more, he sought to confirm it with his next stride—and in that instant, at the sensation of the hairs on his neck standing on end, Heinkel's right hand flew reflexively to his sword hilt.

The lightning draw, the instantaneous movement rooted in survival instinct, stopped. —No, it was stopped. Just as the blade lifted an inch from its sheath.


"—!"


A palm descended from above, pressing down on the pommel.

